From e5ac76922a35fcf8868d3fcb0b38a23f519bf3f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Nintu (agent)" <nintu@simple-host.local>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 21:49:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Setup helper: UpCloud as the recommended place for a small
 box; the agent prompt creates the server there; the installer comes from its
 pinned release
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Where it runs" on the small box's Basics step: UpCloud (recommended, the
default) or a server you already have. UpCloud shows why (the smallest server
runs it; we test on it), a "Create your UpCloud account — $300 in credits"
button to the referral link (new tab, rel=noopener) with the referral note
under it, and the steps. The files step then leads with the agent block: a
one-line command the person runs in their own terminal that reads the API
user's name and password (unechoed) into UPCLOUD_USERNAME/UPCLOUD_PASSWORD,
and a prompt that has their agent use upctl or the API from the environment
only, create the smallest Ubuntu 24.04 server with their SSH key, add the DNS
records (domain and wildcard), run the installer, check /healthz and HTTPS and
report the admin page. The page never asks for UpCloud credentials. TARGETS
now holds both small-box targets, so another platform is one more entry.

The install command (page and prompt) fetches install.sh from the release it
pins instead of main; a test keeps setup.js's INSTALLER_RELEASE equal to
install.sh's VERSION. Pasted output also hides the password in curl -u /
--user (page and server, shared case). The assistant knows the UpCloud path
and its usual errors.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
