index 67963e61..ef446945 100644
--- a/internal/handler/askdata/setup-small-box.txt
+++ b/internal/handler/askdata/setup-small-box.txt
@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@
 A small box is one server running Simple Host with Docker Compose: for self-hosting, a team, a class or a hackathon. It is meant for 1 CPU, 1 GB of RAM and about 25 GB of disk. The installer installs Docker, starts Simple Host with Caddy in front for certificates, prints the admin key once and keeps any settings added to its .env file when it is run again (which is also how you upgrade).
 
+Where to run it. Any fresh Ubuntu server with a public IPv4 address works. The page recommends UpCloud: its smallest server (1 CPU, 1 GB, about $5 a month) runs Simple Host comfortably and is what the project tests on. On the Basics step, "Where it runs" offers UpCloud (recommended) or a server you already have. For UpCloud the page has a "Create your UpCloud account — $300 in credits" button; it is a referral link, and the $300 credit is UpCloud's offer for new accounts through it, on their terms. The steps: create the account, create an API user in the UpCloud control panel (a sub-account with API access), answer the questions on the page, then on the files step run the one line the page gives in your own terminal (it asks for the API user's name and password and keeps them there as environment variables, UPCLOUD_USERNAME and its password) and give your AI agent the prompt. The agent creates the server with upctl or the UpCloud API, adds your SSH key, has you add the DNS records, runs the installer from its pinned release, checks health and HTTPS and reports the admin page. The page never asks for or accepts UpCloud credentials; never ask for them either.
+
 Every default is what simple-host.app itself runs, except two the installer sets for a small disk: the largest site is 100 MB (MAX_ARCHIVE_MB, installer flag --max-site-mb) and one version is kept (KEEP_VERSIONS=1, flag --keep-versions), so there is no rollback unless you keep more.
 
 Addresses. The box needs one domain it owns, like hack.example.com, and a second hostname for published sites, sites.<domain> by default, so a page someone publishes can never script the dashboard. Both point at the server with an A record each; ports 80 and 443 must be open. A small box has no per-person or per-site addresses (they need wildcard certificates): sites live at sites.<domain>/<handle>/<site>/.
diff --git a/internal/handler/askdata/setup-troubleshoot-small-box.txt b/internal/handler/askdata/setup-troubleshoot-small-box.txt
index 1c6ccbc2..b9ed86d8 100644
--- a/internal/handler/askdata/setup-troubleshoot-small-box.txt
+++ b/internal/handler/askdata/setup-troubleshoot-small-box.txt
@@ -11,6 +11,13 @@ The installer:
 - "unknown argument": a flag the installer does not have; the flags are --host, --content, --email, --max-site-mb, --keep-versions.
 - Docker could not be installed: the installer expects a fresh Ubuntu server with sudo.
 
+Creating the server at UpCloud:
+- "Authentication failed" or 401 from upctl or the API: UPCLOUD_USERNAME and UPCLOUD_PASSWORD are not set in the terminal the agent runs in, or name the main account instead of an API user, or the API user lacks API access (control panel: the sub-account's permissions, API connections allowed, and its IP allow-list if one is set). Set them again with the line the page gives, then restart the agent in that terminal.
+- TIER_INVALID when creating the server: the small plans take storage tier standard only; the message names the tier, but the fix is the tier, not the plan.
+- The disk is too small for the template: two templates are named Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS, and the one with NVIDIA drivers and CUDA needs 20 GB. Take the plain one.
+- The plan is not found: plan names change; list the plans and take the smallest with 1 CPU and 1 GB of RAM.
+- SSH times out: use the server's public IPv4 address (access public, family IPv4), not the 10.x utility address, and wait until the server's state is started. If the server has UpCloud's firewall on, allow 22, 80 and 443.
+
 Addresses and certificates:
 - A browser shows a certificate error, or curl says "SSL certificate problem" or "tlsv1 alert internal error": Caddy has not got a certificate for that name. Causes: the name does not point at this server (dig +short <name> must print the server's public IPv4), ports 80 or 443 are closed (Let's Encrypt must reach the server on them; open both in the cloud firewall and on the server), or the name is not the box's own domain or sites hostname (certificates are only issued for names the app confirms). Read the caddy log for the name. After fixing DNS or the firewall, visit the name again; Caddy retries.
 - Too many certificates requested: Let's Encrypt limits repeated requests for the same names; wait an hour before retrying, do not reinstall in a loop.
